|~ `::number`, the kind of numbers, its constructor and its refusal,
   and the verbs of numbers. Each resides on `::qlang/number`, the
   kind whose module declares it, so its subject is a number, and its
   head is its declaration, which the runtime executes before the
   primitive runs. The refusals of a verb sit under it: the head
   raises the one its site declares at the place it checks, and the
   primitive the rest. ~|

::number
  |~~ The kind of a number, a finite double: an infinity or NaN is no
      value, so a step whose result leaves the domain answers an error.
      No kind reads text, so `::number(5)` is `5` and `::number"5"` is
      refused; `parseJson` reads a number out of a string. Its literal
      is JSON's, a leading zero allowed, and its precision the double's own.
      ::snippet~(::number | spec | /verbs) lists its verbs.
      ::link~(::qlang | doc :values)

      ~(5 | type | eq ::number)
      ~(::qlang/number(5) | eq 5)
      A literal past the range is no number the parser reads:
      ~("1e400" | parse !| type | eq ::ParseError)
      A step whose result leaves the range answers an error:
      ~(1e308 | mul 10 !| type | eq ::MulResultNotFiniteError)
      An integer and a decimal are one kind, rounded as a double rounds:
      ~([-1 2.5e-3 (0.1 | add 0.2)] | eq [-1 0.0025 0.30000000000000004])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{:impl :qlang/type/number}

::NumberPayloadNotNumberError
  |~~ `::number` reads its payload as the number it is; a payload of
      another kind is refused, and text is read by `parseJson`.
      ~(
        ::number"5"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::NumberPayloadNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

:add
  |~~ Adds two numbers: `a | add b` computes a + b, and the full form
      `add a b` reads both modifiers against the value in the pipe.

      ~(10 | add 3 | eq 13)
      ~({:x 10 :y 3} | add /x /y | eq 13)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:addend ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/add})

::AddLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ `add` adds numbers: a vector adds element by element, `* add 1`,
      and sums by `sum`; a string is joined by `append`.

      ~(
        "hello" | add 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AddLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::AddRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The addend of `add` is a number; two fields of one map add by
      `add /price /tax`, both read from it.

      ~(
        1 | add "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AddRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::AddResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `add` answered outside the finite doubles a number of qlang
      is: both operands are numbers, held as `:leftValue` and
      `:rightValue`, and the size of the answer is what the site
      refuses.

      ~(
        1e308 | add 1e308
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::AddResultNotFiniteError 1e308]
       )
  ~~|

:sub
  |~~ Subtracts the subtrahend from the subject, the minuend.

      ~(10 | sub 3 | eq 7)
      ~({:x 10 :y 3} | sub /x /y | eq 7)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:subtrahend ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/sub})

::SubLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ `sub` subtracts from a number; a vector subtracts element by
      element, `* sub 1`.

      ~(
        "hello" | sub 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::SubLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::SubRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The subtrahend of `sub` is a number; two fields of one map
      subtract by `sub /total /paid`.

      ~(
        1 | sub "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::SubRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::SubResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `sub` answered outside the finite doubles a number of qlang
      is: both operands are numbers, held as `:leftValue` and
      `:rightValue`, and the size of the answer is what the site
      refuses.

      ~(
        -1e308 | sub 1e308
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::SubResultNotFiniteError -1e308]
       )
  ~~|

:mul
  |~~ Multiplies two numbers.

      ~(10 | mul 3 | eq 30)
      ~({:x 5 :y 4} | mul /x /y | eq 20)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:multiplier ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/mul})

::MulLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ `mul` multiplies numbers; a vector multiplies element by element,
      `* mul 2`.

      ~(
        "hello" | mul 2
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::MulLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::MulRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The multiplier of `mul` is a number; two fields of one map
      multiply by `mul /price /qty`.

      ~(
        1 | mul "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::MulRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::MulResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `mul` answered outside the finite doubles a number of qlang
      is: both operands are numbers, held as `:leftValue` and
      `:rightValue`, and the size of the answer is what the site
      refuses.

      ~(
        1e308 | mul 10
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::MulResultNotFiniteError 1e308]
       )
  ~~|

:div
  |~~ Divides the subject, the dividend, by the divisor, and refuses a
      divisor of zero with `::DivisionByZeroError`.

      ~(10 | div 2 | eq 5)
      ~({:x 20 :y 4} | div /x /y | eq 5)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:divisor ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/div})

::DivLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ `div` divides a number; a vector divides element by element,
      `* div 2`.

      ~(
        "hello" | div 2
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::DivLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::DivRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The divisor of `div` is a number; two fields of one map divide by
      `div /sum /count`.

      ~(
        1 | div "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::DivRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::DivResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `div` answered outside the finite doubles a number of qlang
      is: both operands are numbers, held as `:leftValue` and
      `:rightValue`, and the size of the answer is what the site
      refuses.

      ~(
        1e308 | div 0.0001
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::DivResultNotFiniteError 1e308]
       )
  ~~|

::DivisionByZeroError
  |~~ A division by zero has no finite answer; a divisor that may be
      zero is tested first, `if (/count | eq 0) ~(0) ~(div /sum /count)`.

      ~(
        10 | div 0
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::DivisionByZeroError]
       )
  ~~|

:round
  |~~ The number rounded half away from zero to the digits after the
      point the call gives, none when it gives none; a negative count
      rounds to tens and beyond.

      ~(3.14159 | round 2 | eq 3.14)
      ~(2.5 | round | eq 3)
      ~(-2.5 | round | eq -3)
      ~(1234 | round -2 | eq 1200)
      ~(-0.4 | round | print | eq "0")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:digits 0 | :returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/round})

::RoundSubjectNotNumberError
  |~~ `round` rounds a number; a string holding one is read first by
      `parseJson`.
      ~(
        "2.5" | round
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::RoundSubjectNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::RoundDigitsNotIntegerError
  |~~ The digits of `round` are a whole number, `round 2`.
      ~(
        2.5 | round 0.5
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::RoundDigitsNotIntegerError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:gt
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than the number the call gives.

      ~(10 | gt 5 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gt})

:gte
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than or equal to the number the
      call gives.

      ~(10 | gte 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gte})

:lt
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than the number the call gives.

      ~(5 | lt 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lt})

:lte
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than or equal to the number the call
      gives.

      ~(10 | lte 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lte})
